  "observations": [
    "The boundary-preserving numerical scalar path exhausted its shared RK budget on a valid constant-speed batch of 65,537 queries. After numerical checkpoint acceptance, affine inversion now handles constant-speed intervals directly. The full capacity of 1,048,576 queries is exercised by a regression test.",
    "Unequal-weight rational lines have varying speed despite their straight images and remain excluded from the affine inverse. Their existing quadrature behavior is retained.",
    "Equivalent NURBS and PolylineCurve sources have different scalar stopping rules in the measured native cases. NURBS may accept a nearby natural knot; PolylineCurve scalar and batch queries retain the exact clock. Analytic constant-speed sources use their exact clock as well.",
    "New source-type comparisons cover 144 scalar queries and 216 polyline batch queries. The previous 4,356 strict NURBS scalar comparisons remain passing after affine inversion replaces unnecessary integration work.",
    "Original and simplified source types produce identical fitted definitions for both single and paired preparation in the six measured profile sets. Their clock difference alone does not establish adaptive subdivision policy. No new adaptive command fits were admitted.",
    "The existing general scalar SDK counterexamples and unfinished curved-source fitting policy remain separate limitations. No native epsilon or fitting-distance admission policy was relaxed."
  ],
